("the skill weights are \\tbc{0.37} for CRU~TS4, \\tbc{0.26} for HARv2, \\tbc{0.22} for ERA5, \\tbc{0.09} for CHELSA-W5E5 and \\tbc{0.07} for ERA5-Land (Table~S8): the CRU family receives about \\tbc{0.46} and the ERA5 family about \\tbc{0.54} of the weight. In the leave-one-glacier-out test, the weighted ensemble predicts the field data better than equal family weights (RMSE \\tbc{0.503} against \\tbc{0.518}\\,m\\,w.e.\\,yr$^{-1}$, anomaly correlation \\tbc{0.61} against \\tbc{0.59}). In the leave-one-sub-region-out split test the two are practically equal (area-weighted mean absolute error \\tbc{0.0514} against \\tbc{0.0517}\\,m\\,w.e.\\,yr$^{-1}$), and both are better than equal member weights (\\tbc{0.0622}).",
 "the skill weights are 0.37 for CRU~TS4, 0.25 for HARv2, 0.22 for ERA5, 0.09 for CHELSA-W5E5 and 0.07 for ERA5-Land (Table~S8): the CRU family receives about 0.46 and the ERA5 family about 0.54 of the weight. In the leave-one-glacier-out test, the weighted ensemble predicts the field data better than equal family weights (RMSE 0.502 against 0.516\\,m\\,w.e.\\,yr$^{-1}$, anomaly correlation 0.60 against 0.59). In the leave-one-sub-region-out split test the two are practically equal (area-weighted mean absolute error 0.0514 against 0.0517\\,m\\,w.e.\\,yr$^{-1}$), and both are better than equal member weights (0.0623)."),
